What does a red accent do to attention and to the viewer
title end

section start
title start
Abstract
title end
paragraph start
Question: what does a red accent do to attention and to the viewer. Method: a review of colour and psychological functioning and a review of effects on thinking and emotion. Result: red gets a measurable attention advantage and raises arousal more than green or blue at equal lightness and saturation, but effects on performance are mixed and some did not replicate. Limit: all findings come from lab studies, not social video.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Introduction
title end
paragraph start
Red is allowed only when needed, so this page asks what red does.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Methods
title end
paragraph start
Read the two reviews and a popular summary, collecting findings on attention, arousal, performance and meaning.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Results
title end
paragraph start
Red stimuli receive an attentional advantage in visual search studies, and blue light was linked to greater alertness (Elliot, 2015).
paragraph end
paragraph start
At equal lightness and saturation, red causes higher arousal than green or blue, and vivid colours cause more arousal than pale ones (von Castell and co-authors, 2018).
paragraph end
paragraph start
Brief exposure to red worsened performance in some anagram studies, but other attempts to replicate such effects failed (von Castell and co-authors, 2018).
paragraph end
paragraph start
The meaning of red depends on context: it can convey anger, embarrassment or attraction (Elliot, 2015).
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Discussion
title end
paragraph start
Red used sparingly is supported as a cue that pulls attention. Large red areas have no support either way. Keep red for moments that need an alert feeling, such as breaking news or a warning.
paragraph end
paragraph start
todo: test one red accent against none on the same series
Put it in the experiments log.
paragraph end
section end

